#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124313 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124504 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "945f684f-9707-5a0a-b5d7-0753f11b4385" :question/math-academy-id "e-5266" :question/problem "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is \n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =4{x}^{2}+8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/8} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0).$  Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124314 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "7e876c7b-849f-59ce-a654-881ec9464475" :question/math-academy-id "e-5269" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-1) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124365 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/id #uuid "5ce5b329-ac5d-5581-b396-eadd569b9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-6087" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+5) ^2}{3} = z-1\n$$\n\nand the plane $x=2$  onto the $yz$-plane is a parabola. Find the vertex of this parabola." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $x=2,$ we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3}(y+5)^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $yz$-plane, we get the equation of a parabola that has its vertex at $(-5,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124504 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22db86a0-af60-573d-a69e-6a16f625736a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281996} {:answer/id #uuid "414049e5-9a1e-58de-9e59-c67f5cf5b695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282250} {:answer/id #uuid "8965b26c-2b11-514f-b935-967dfed26d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282875} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7b60de-2bc0-5339-8171-b43cd56796f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283738} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4ec42c-6ee4-5063-816f-832af8ab884d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8965b26c-2b11-514f-b935-967dfed26d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282875} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0fef52-7d48-577d-9116-6f8431acd38c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cb8c0a4-a2ed-5b52-8a78-e703db55698d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164265" :question/problem "Find the vertex of the elliptic paraboloid $3z+6=3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+6 & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ 3(z+2) & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z+2 & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-2) & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(3,-2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124504 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "390f4661-5a0f-5b3f-b25c-eaa8c3ef06ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282191} {:answer/id #uuid "6e27f0c3-12ef-5c96-a5ea-3ba6977f8de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=3 \\\\ z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282634} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7a600c-728d-58b5-a4ed-d9bf939a6bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=3 \\\\ z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283181} {:answer/id #uuid "b04ade40-7746-536a-a6b3-6bb9700c6ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283213} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ce0e84-2eab-5071-9566-0c4afa412297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e27f0c3-12ef-5c96-a5ea-3ba6977f8de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=3 \\\\ z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282634} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bbe4e29-00c0-5dd6-8a3d-4a02a12fec6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03b953d0-f6a1-54cc-956c-27178aec298e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164269" :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{6}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nx-0=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-(-1))^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $x$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,3,-1).$\n Since it is oriented along the $x$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=3 \\\\ z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124504 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce7234f-5256-51b7-b0d3-1bdc9634780f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4 \\\\ z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282352} {:answer/id #uuid "65306c60-33c0-5067-b974-2cdce7630954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4 \\\\ y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282559} {:answer/id #uuid "94292dc9-15fd-5e22-a29f-23f3fc4447be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282961} {:answer/id #uuid "a21f1dcf-7d3c-5e16-a07a-73ec84e9c876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-1 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283105} {:answer/id #uuid "b54cc70b-3647-51e8-8669-18163028be78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4 \\\\ y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94292dc9-15fd-5e22-a29f-23f3fc4447be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282961} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ad3007-7ce0-5c8a-baeb-d70b971b8014" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7722e4d9-5bec-545d-832c-cb50e7715d46" :question/math-academy-id "q-164270" :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $y+1=\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\ny-(-1)=\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $y$-axis that has its vertex at $(-4,-1,2).$\n Since it is oriented along the $y$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a796a1f-19d5-5d90-9666-c2e714900ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $x=\\pm (z+5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282205} {:answer/id #uuid "650e88df-7183-58eb-ac01-fd91f1dfaae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: ${x}^{2}-3{z}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282556} {:answer/id #uuid "70d1e82e-f67c-5cc8-a85a-e587ee7c8597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $x=2(z\\pm 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282654} {:answer/id #uuid "ce09e96b-5b2f-5f32-b542-fc17e5f3d612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: ${x}^{2}=2(z+2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283490} {:answer/id #uuid "e341391a-654f-5899-971b-749cbc08d629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: ${x}^{2}=2(z+5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e341391a-654f-5899-971b-749cbc08d629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: ${x}^{2}=2(z+5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283669} :answer-field/id #uuid "c95cde96-fbf2-54b2-b3e6-0d0df0cb14cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7539eac9-bac1-5e13-b04d-8ad08919bed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-164279" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+2=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+2 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z+2 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ z+2 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3 \\\\ z+5 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2(z+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $xz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e555ae3-3fc5-583e-aafa-04f2edeb4618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-6, 0, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121989} {:answer/id #uuid "beda99dd-71d2-5c78-9628-0aa258d2193a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1, 0, 0)$ and $(11, 0, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121990} {:answer/id #uuid "00975b63-2032-532e-915b-82e2d6fe5ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0, 0)$ and $(4, 0, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121991} {:answer/id #uuid "38f6d984-8efa-5e7c-9219-b04f28ac2cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-6, 0, 0)$ and $(6, 0, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121992} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4c51fa-03df-575e-9801-829637f28c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 0, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121993}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bb717c66-f216-5306-85da-ab3c60ee5cd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121988}] :question/id #uuid "0e528ebf-cade-5a11-bb2e-6b398c870406" :question/math-academy-id "q-35705" :question/problem "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x - 5)^{2} - 8(y + 2)^{2} = 